   "limitations": "Not compared with an emulator; the fight was not played to its end in game (its death and drops are checked by a test). Not ported: the minimap chunk update and the statue opening cutscene of its death, the in-game timer at bosses. The runtime's camera is wider than the GBA screen and only centred on gLockScreen's position (not the native scrolling); BGCNT_LOW_PRIORITY sprites are drawn after BG3 and before the other backgrounds; SSC_HURTS_KNOCKBACK_IF_INVINCIBLE's hurt knocks Samus back even against a wall. Kraid locks the room, rises with its BG2 body (gBg2Movement, the room's BG_PROP_MOVING BG2) for two seconds with ceiling debris and shakes, then steps left and right with the multi-sprite tables (sKraidMultiSpriteData_*, exported from the decompilation with their part offsets), breaking the floor blocks in its way and stomping with dust; the camera follows gLockScreen five blocks left of Samus. Its head opens on its own or for three seconds after a hit on the shut mouth; missiles, super missiles and charged beams flying left into the open mouth hurt it, other shots vanish and missiles bounce off. Under 3/4, 1/3 and 1/4 of its health its sprite palette row and BG palette row 10 (sKraidPal rows 5 to 7) change, it swipes at Samus near on its right, steps faster, and its spikes shoot from the bottom, middle and (at low health) top holes, break the platforms on their way, carry Samus and stick in the wall six seconds; its left arm throws two nails toward Samus when fewer than three drops (one on easy) exist. Its body hurts and stops Samus (SSC_HURTS_KNOCKBACK_IF_INVINCIBLE), its spikes hurt from the right and carry her on top (SSC_HURTS_RIGHT_CAN_STAND_ON_TOP); the right arm and foot are drawn behind BG2 (BGCNT_LOW_PRIORITY). Its death sets EVENT_KRAID_KILLED, sinks it with explosions and dust, keeps Samus out of it, then PE_MAIN_BOSS_DEATH, the boss killed music after a fade (FadeCurrentMusicAndQueueNextMusic), BG2 hidden and every part exploding with its drops. Checked by capture in kraid_030 from the right platforms: missiles fired standing hit the left arm and bounce off, jumping ones reach the mouth (a super missile took it from 300 to 200 health), and the body and the sprites change colour together (the runtime picks the /rowN looks of Kraid's health rows). The blocks its spikes and feet break vanish from BG1 as well (CAA_REMOVE_SOLID through the block engine): the middle spike reaching the wall clears the platform above it, and Samus falls through the gap, as in the game.",
