   "limitations": "The screens render at the native 240x160 size, fitted to the runtime view. Not compared with an emulator. MzmScreen holds what the menus and cutscenes share: the GbaPpu they draw on, gOamData and gNextOamSlot, the *_NonGameplay background offsets and blending values, the EWRAM work area and gChangedInput / gButtonInput in GBA key bits. ProcessMenuOam runs the menu object animations from the ROM's struct OamArray and FrameData tables with all their pre-actions and writes the OAM parts at the object's position minus its bound background's offset, with its priority and object mode; ResetFreeOam, UpdateMenuOamDataId, ClearGfxRam and ApplySpecialBackgroundFadingColor (in, flash, out and the unnamed fade) are ported; MenuOamData records are read from their 16-byte ROM layout. ProcessComplexMenuOam is ported (mzm_complex_menu_oam_process): ProcessMenuOam with an object's rotationScaling sent through ProcessComplexOam (double-sized, matrix 0) with gCurrentOamRotation and gCurrentOamScaling; the game over screen draws its head with it (never rotated, so placed as ProcessMenuOam places it). A bank-backed test checks both cases. The helpers run on every captured non-gameplay screen (the title screen, the file select, the game over, the cutscenes).",
