   "limitations": "Runs the pinned Aria ROM headless through the mGBA core API (aos_mgba_session): frame stepping, scripted key masks, PPM screenshots, save states and WRAM peeks. aos_lab_worker records state blobs, runs scripted actions with parameter jitter against memory-probe evals, and accumulates per-parameter success weights in a local SQLite database (aos_lab_db, WAL). aos_lab_viewer is a GTK4 registry that polls trials, weights, states and runs live, with a bandit action selector, guaranteed randomized inputs, novelty auto-checkpoints, verified WRAM probes (position/HP/MP/gold), an autonomous scout mode that plays the early game and validates free-gameplay checkpoints, plus a WRAM reward layer (new rooms/scenes/items/souls/level-ups/clean traversals vs damage/death) driving the bandit, with waypoint restarts from random checkpoints and a VRAM tilemap signature per trial (first OCR brick), worker heartbeat/stuck detection with a viewer STALE flag, and a DB purge for dead weight and eight colour-coded parallel worker slots (per-worker pause/resume/stop, batch spawning, save-state recording, per-worker speed caps) and an in-memory live log. Workers publish live 240x160 framebuffers over POSIX shm shown as a draggable 4x2 video grid (aspect-locked), with a global fleet bar for pause-all/stop-all/global speed, and every trial stores a visual scene signature (tile hash, HUD hash, text-ink rows, tile count, RGB histogram) in its metrics JSON. Verified end-to-end: state capture, trial batches, weight updates and shm video. Still open: calibrated gameplay probes (player position/HP addresses), menu-navigation scripts for the scrolling intro, vendored mGBA sources and a spawn-batch button in the viewer.",
